    "content": "Mogadishu — Somalia's Finance Minister, Biixi Imaan Cige, addressed the 8th Korea-Africa Summit in Seoul on Wednesday, presenting the country's vision for a digital-led economic transformation. The summit, themed around using artificial intelligence to bolster economic infrastructure and speed up Africa's transition, drew finance ministers from across the continent.\n\nMinister Cige emphasized how AI and modern technologies can help Somalia leapfrog traditional development barriers, especially in sectors like financial services and public administration. He called for stronger Korea-Africa partnerships to support digital capacity building and investment in tech-enabled infrastructure.\n\nThe minister's remarks come as Somalia continues to rebuild state institutions after decades of conflict, with economic recovery hinging on embracing innovation. The country has seen growing mobile money penetration and a budding tech startup scene, which officials hope to link with international partners like South Korea.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which plays a crucial role in remittances and investment, a formal push toward digital economy policies could translate into new opportunities for cross-border business and fintech collaborations. Diaspora entrepreneurs have already pioneered e-money services in Somalia, and closer ties with Asian tech hubs may open further avenues.\n\nSouth Korea's own journey from aid recipient to tech powerhouse offers a template for emerging economies, and Somali officials are keen to attract Korean expertise and financing. The summit is expected to yield concrete cooperation frameworks in the coming months, though no specific projects have been announced yet.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/09/soomaaliya-oo-shirka-kuuriya-iyo-afrika-ku-soo-bandhigtay-himiladeeda-dhaqaalaha-dijitaalka-ah/",
