    "original_title": "FAO iyo SoDMA Ka Wada Hadlay U Diyaargarowga Fatahaadaha Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "fao-and-somali-disaster-agency-discuss-flood-preparedness-measures",
    "summary": "In Mogadishu, the UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and Somalia's National Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA) held talks on September 9, 2026, to coordinate efforts to mitigate potential flooding risks along the Jubba and Shabelle rivers.",
    "content": "In a meeting held in Mogadishu on September 9, 2026, the UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and Somalia's National Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA) discussed strategies to prepare for potential floods. FAO's Country Representative, Etienne Peterschmitt, met with SoDMA Commissioner Mahmud Macallin Abdulle to address humanitarian concerns and risks posed by expected heavy rains.\n\nThe talks focused on proactive measures to protect communities, farmlands, and livelihoods situated along the Jubba and Shabelle rivers. Both parties emphasized strengthening river defenses and closing vulnerable points where water could breach embankments. The collaboration aims to minimize damage from seasonal flooding that has historically affected central and southern Somalia.\n\nSomalia has experienced recurrent flooding events in recent years, causing displacement, crop destruction, and disrupting access to clean water and health services. The partnership between FAO and SoDMA forms part of broader preparedness efforts that involve early warning systems and coordinated response mechanisms, with support from international humanitarian organizations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are directly relevant: many diaspora members maintain economic and family ties to agricultural regions vulnerable to flooding. Remittances often flow to these areas to assist relatives during emergencies, and the diaspora frequently contributes to recovery and rebuilding efforts. Improved preparedness and risk reduction help mitigate the impacts of such disasters, reducing the frequency and severity of humanitarian crises.\n\nThe exact timeline for implementing the discussed measures was not detailed, but both agencies expressed commitment to further collaboration and to keeping communities informed. This meeting underscores the importance of proactive disaster management to safeguard lives and livelihoods in Somalia.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/09/fao-iyo-sodma-ka-wada-hadlay-u-diyaargarowga-fatahaadaha-soomaaliya/",
